d  a resolution condemning the the the  thrust of our reporting but not finding  any errors in the stories that we wrote  they actually passed a resolution  denouncing you yes the township passed a  resolution denouncing us but important  to note found no error in the story that  we wrote about what happened in their  town  but the series had huge impact including  from the story of an officer killed  off-duty in a car crash I found out that  he was drunk and on drugs at the time  found out that his department didn't  have a policy for random drug testing so  we requested drug testing policies from  every department in the state found that  more than 100 departments did not have a  policy for random drug testing and  police officers this sounds like the  kind of thing that any good local  investigative team could do anywhere in  the country that's absolutely right and  you don't have to be an investigative  reporter to do this kind of work  investigative journalism is alive and  well in New Jersey at the Asbury Park  Press and at the other papers in the USA  Today Network we're all working together  out here and we've continued to have  follow-up stories so stick with us and  we'll keep it going  so to Andrew Ford and his colleagues in  this dynamic editor Paul Dunn pro-c a  big shout out for letting its Frida's  and New Jersey taxpayers in on the 60  million dollar hush money secret that's  our program for this week we hope to see  you back here again next week  you
 

